{{ if ne .chezmoi.os "darwin" -}}
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# cisco-vpn — Cisco AnyConnect VPN with Entra ID (SAML) SSO, from this *headless* Lima VM.
# LINUX ONLY: like pbcopy, this is a chezmoi template that renders empty on darwin —
# a Mac has a real browser (and Cisco Secure Client), so the console dance below is
# pointless there, and macOS packages belong in the Brewfile anyway.
#
# Why this works with no browser in the VM: openconnect >= 9.00 speaks the AnyConnect
# "external browser" SAML flow (--external-browser). It hands the IdP URL to a
# "browser" executable (exec'd directly, URL as $1 — a shell script works) and
# listens on loopback port 29786 ([::1] on 9.12) for the completion redirect. Lima
# auto-forwards every guest loopback listener — ::1 included — to 127.0.0.1 on the
# macOS host, so the URL is opened in Safari/Chrome ON THE MAC, Entra ID (incl.
# MFA/Conditional Access) runs there, and the browser's final localhost:29786
# redirect lands back inside the VM, straight into openconnect. Our "browser" is a
# stub that prints the URL — and drops it on the Mac clipboard through the pty
# (osc52-copy), so it's a Cmd-V away. Inside tmux, prefix+Tab (extrakto) also grabs
# it off the screen. Even if the stub failed, openconnect prints the URL itself and
# keeps listening. (On a non-Lima remote box the same flow works — bridge the
# redirect yourself with: ssh -L 29786:localhost:29786 <box>.)
#
# Server-side requirement: the ASA/FTD tunnel-group must allow external-browser SAML
# ("authentication saml external-browser", ASA 9.17(1)+/FTD 7.1+). Head-ends locked
# to the default *embedded* webview flow abort with "No SSO handler" on the CLI —
# console SSO can't work there (that flow needs a GUI webview, e.g. openconnect-sso).
set -euo pipefail

have() { command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1; }

# One cleanup list, one trap. Separate `trap … EXIT` calls would silently replace
# each other, and this script now makes up to two temp files in a single run.
CLEANUP_FILES=()
cleanup() { rm -f ${CLEANUP_FILES+"${CLEANUP_FILES[@]}"}; }
trap cleanup EXIT

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
usage: cisco-vpn install                        one-time: openconnect (>= 9.00) + vpnc-scripts
       cisco-vpn <gateway> [openconnect args]   connect; SSO URL opens in a browser ON YOUR MAC
       cisco-vpn sso <gateway> [saml args]      same, for *embedded*-SAML head-ends
       cisco-vpn cookie <host> [fingerprint]    connect with an auth cookie read from stdin
       CISCO_VPN_SERVER=<gateway> cisco-vpn     gateway from the env instead

Running it as a service:
       cisco-vpn sso <gateway> -b               connect, then detach into the background
       cisco-vpn status [-v]                    connected? interface, routes, DNS, traffic
       cisco-vpn health [-q]                    is traffic actually FLOWING through it?
       cisco-vpn heal [--auto]                  …and suspend+resume it if not
       cisco-vpn suspend                        drop the tunnel but KEEP the session
       cisco-vpn resume [-b]                    reconnect from the saved session, no login
       cisco-vpn stop                           disconnect AND log out (discards the session)
       cisco-vpn install-service                resume on boot + minutely heal watchdog

suspend/resume exist because of how openconnect handles signals: SIGINT logs the
session off (the cookie dies), SIGHUP does not (the cookie stays usable). So `stop`
is a real logout, while `suspend` — and a reboot via the systemd unit — leaves a
session you can resume without touching a browser. Sessions still expire server-side.

health exists because a host sleep often leaves the tunnel LOOKING up — process
alive, tun0 present, routes installed — while nothing flows; only a probe through
the tunnel tells those states apart. Targets come from $CISCO_VPN_PROBE or
~/.config/dotenv/vpn-probe (host[:port], [v6]:port, URL or dns:<ip>, one per line;
any one answering = healthy) and default to the DNS servers the VPN itself pushed.
heal is that probe plus the fix you would type by hand (suspend && resume); the
watchdog timer installed by install-service runs it minutely. It knows the
difference between a wedge and everything else: while the GATEWAY is unreachable
(uplink down, Mac asleep) it waits without acting, a young openconnect with no tun
yet is a login in progress, `systemctl stop cisco-vpn` and `cisco-vpn suspend` are
respected, reconnects are capped at 3/hour, and after 3 failed attempts in a row
it parks itself — status says so — until a manual heal/resume/sso.

The gateway must come first; everything after it is passed through to openconnect
(e.g. cisco-vpn vpn.example.com --authgroup=Employees). Ctrl-C disconnects.

Use `sso` when plain mode dies with "No SSO handler" (e.g. vpn.unisg.ch): it drives
the login through openconnect-saml --headless, which prints a URL you open on the
Mac exactly like the native flow. One-time: pipx install openconnect-saml.

For a hands-free `sso`, load the browser extension in browser-extension/vpn-token-relay
(repo root, "Load unpacked" in chrome://extensions on the Mac). It spots the token
cookie and posts it to the callback below by itself — no bookmarklet, no pasting.

`cookie` is the manual variant — authenticate anywhere (incl. on the Mac with
`openconnect-saml --authenticate`), then pipe the cookie in:
  echo "$COOKIE" | cisco-vpn cookie "$HOST" "$FINGERPRINT"

Route control (works in every mode):
  --routes 10.1.0.0/16,192.168.50.0/24   send ONLY these through the tunnel
  --no-vpn-dns                           keep this VM's DNS; don't take the VPN's
  ~/.config/dotenv/vpn-routes            the same list, one CIDR per line, set once
  CISCO_VPN_ROUTES / CISCO_VPN_DNS=off   env equivalents
Without --routes you get whatever the head-end pushes — which for a full-tunnel
group means everything, including traffic that has no business on the corporate net.
With systemd-resolved present, VPN DNS is applied per-link (split DNS): only the
VPN's domains query its resolvers, public lookups stay on the local resolver, and
a wedged tunnel no longer takes every lookup down with it.
EOF
  exit "${1:-0}"
}

SUDO=""; [ "$(id -u)" -ne 0 ] && SUDO="sudo"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- route control
# openconnect hands the server's config to vpnc-script through the environment;
# vpnc-script installs a route per CISCO_SPLIT_INC_<n>_* triple, and falls back to
# a DEFAULT route through the tunnel when CISCO_SPLIT_INC is unset. So the clean
# place to narrow a tunnel is a shim in front of vpnc-script that overwrites those
# variables — no post-connect `ip route` racing, and it re-applies on reconnect.
# Setting a smaller count also masks any extra ranges the server pushed, since
# vpnc-script only reads indices 0..CISCO_SPLIT_INC-1.
masklen_to_mask() {
  local m="$1" o="" i part
  for i in 1 2 3 4; do
    if [ "$m" -ge 8 ]; then part=255; m=$((m - 8))
    else
      case "$m" in
        0) part=0 ;;   1) part=128 ;; 2) part=192 ;; 3) part=224 ;;
        4) part=240 ;; 5) part=248 ;; 6) part=252 ;; 7) part=254 ;;
      esac
      m=0
    fi
    o="$o.$part"
  done
  printf '%s' "${o#.}"
}

find_vpnc_script() {
  local c
  for c in /usr/share/vpnc-scripts/vpnc-script /etc/vpnc/vpnc-script /usr/local/etc/vpnc/vpnc-script; do
    [ -x "$c" ] && { printf '%s' "$c"; return 0; }
  done
  return 1
}

# Emits the connect shim's path via ROUTE_SCRIPT; callers pass it to openconnect
# as --script. The shim lives at a STABLE path, not a mktemp in /tmp: openconnect
# re-execs it on every DPD reconnect and on the final disconnect, so it must
# outlive this wrapper (the EXIT trap used to delete it) — and systemd-tmpfiles
# ages /tmp at 10 days, under the 14-day life of a session. Each connect
# overwrites it.
build_route_script() {
  local real f cidr addr len mask rev_zones="" o1 o2 o3 o4 _rest dns_extra dns_defroute i=0
  real="$(find_vpnc_script)" || {
    echo "cisco-vpn: no vpnc-script found — install vpnc-scripts (cisco-vpn install)" >&2
    return 1
  }
  mkdir -p "$STATE_DIR"
  f="$STATE_DIR/vpn-connect-script"
  ROUTE_SCRIPT="$f"
  {
    echo '#!/bin/sh'
    echo '# generated by cisco-vpn — narrows the tunnel and places DNS, then defers to vpnc-script'
    if [ -n "$ROUTES" ]; then
      for cidr in $ROUTES; do
        addr="${cidr%%/*}"; len="${cidr#*/}"
        [ "$len" = "$cidr" ] && len=32          # a bare host address
        mask="$(masklen_to_mask "$len")"
        echo "CISCO_SPLIT_INC_${i}_ADDR=$addr;    export CISCO_SPLIT_INC_${i}_ADDR"
        echo "CISCO_SPLIT_INC_${i}_MASK=$mask;    export CISCO_SPLIT_INC_${i}_MASK"
        echo "CISCO_SPLIT_INC_${i}_MASKLEN=$len;  export CISCO_SPLIT_INC_${i}_MASKLEN"
        i=$((i + 1))
        # Reverse zones for the narrowed ranges (rounded out to octet boundaries)
        # so in-tunnel PTR lookups also reach the VPN resolver under split DNS.
        IFS=. read -r o1 o2 o3 _rest <<<"$addr"
        if   [ "$len" -ge 24 ]; then rev_zones="$rev_zones ~$o3.$o2.$o1.in-addr.arpa"
        elif [ "$len" -ge 16 ]; then rev_zones="$rev_zones ~$o2.$o1.in-addr.arpa"
        elif [ "$len" -ge  8 ]; then rev_zones="$rev_zones ~$o1.in-addr.arpa"
        fi
      done
      echo "CISCO_SPLIT_INC=$i; export CISCO_SPLIT_INC"
    fi
    if [ "$VPN_DNS" = off ]; then
      echo 'unset INTERNAL_IP4_DNS CISCO_DEF_DOMAIN'
      echo "[ \"\${reason:-}\" != connect ] || rm -f '$STATE_DIR/vpn.dns'"
      echo "exec '$real' \"\$@\""
    else
      # Split DNS through systemd-resolved instead of letting vpnc-script replace
      # /etc/resolv.conf wholesale. Two outages hang off that file: vpnc-script
      # writes THROUGH the /etc/resolv.conf symlink into resolved's own stub file,
      # which resolved regenerates at will — silently dropping the VPN DNS while
      # the tunnel is up (the "lose DNS from time to time") — and a wholesale
      # replacement points EVERY lookup, public included, at resolvers reachable
      # only through the tunnel, so any wedge kills all DNS. Per-link config dies
      # with the tun device, so disconnect needs no restore. When resolved is not
      # answering (other distros, early boot), INTERNAL_IP4_DNS stays set and
      # vpnc-script's own file handling takes over as before. vpn.dns records
      # "the server pushed resolvers and we kept them" for run_health.
      if [ -n "$ROUTES" ]; then
        dns_extra="$rev_zones"; dns_defroute=no
      else
        # Full tunnel: everything routes through the VPN anyway — make its
        # resolvers the lookup default too (still per-link, still immune to
        # resolved regenerating its stub file).
        dns_extra=" '~.'"; dns_defroute=yes
      fi
      cat <<EOF
vpndns=""; vpndomain=""
if [ "\${reason:-}" = connect ]; then
  if [ -n "\${INTERNAL_IP4_DNS:-}" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "\$INTERNAL_IP4_DNS" > '$STATE_DIR/vpn.dns'
    if command -v resolvectl >/dev/null 2>&1 && resolvectl status >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      vpndns="\$INTERNAL_IP4_DNS"; vpndomain="\${CISCO_DEF_DOMAIN:-}"
      unset INTERNAL_IP4_DNS CISCO_DEF_DOMAIN
    fi
  else
    rm -f '$STATE_DIR/vpn.dns'
  fi
fi
'$real' "\$@"; rc=\$?
if [ -n "\$vpndns" ] && [ -n "\${TUNDEV:-}" ]; then
  resolvectl dns "\$TUNDEV" \$vpndns 2>/dev/null || true
  resolvectl domain "\$TUNDEV" \$vpndomain$dns_extra 2>/dev/null || true
  resolvectl default-route "\$TUNDEV" $dns_defroute 2>/dev/null || true
fi
exit \$rc
EOF
    fi
  } > "$f"
  chmod 755 "$f"
  own_state "$STATE_DIR" "$f"
}

validate_routes() {
  local cidr
  for cidr in $ROUTES; do
    case "$cidr" in
      *[!0-9./]*|""|/*|*/) echo "cisco-vpn: bad route '$cidr' (want a.b.c.d/len)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
  done
}

# Applied to every connect path, so `sso`, `cookie` and the native flow behave the
# same. Sets ROUTE_ARGS, which each openconnect invocation splices in. The shim is
# built even without --routes — it is also what carries the DNS placement.
ROUTE_ARGS=(); ROUTE_SCRIPT=""
setup_routes() {
  local file="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/dotenv/vpn-routes"
  if [ -z "$ROUTES" ] && [ -r "$file" ]; then
    ROUTES="$(sed 's/#.*//' "$file" | tr '\n' ' ')"
  fi
  ROUTES="$(printf '%s' "${ROUTES//,/ }" | tr -s '[:space:]' ' ')"
  ROUTES="${ROUTES# }"; ROUTES="${ROUTES% }"
  [ -n "$ROUTES" ] && validate_routes
  build_route_script || exit 1
  ROUTE_ARGS=(--script "$ROUTE_SCRIPT")
  [ -n "$ROUTES" ] && echo "==> cisco-vpn: tunnelling only: $ROUTES"
  [ "$VPN_DNS" = off ] && echo "    keeping this VM's DNS"
  return 0   # the guarded echoes above can end on a failing test; see connect_with_cookie
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------ session / background
# openconnect's signals are what make all of this work (see SIGNALS in its man page):
#   SIGINT/SIGTERM  clean shutdown — LOGS THE SESSION OFF, so the cookie dies with it
#   SIGHUP          disconnects but does NOT log off — the cookie stays resumable
#   SIGUSR1         dumps connection info + statistics to openconnect's output
STATE_DIR="${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/dotenv"
# NOT in $STATE_DIR: systemd refuses a root service's PIDFile whose path crosses
# user-owned directories ("unsafe symlink chain"), which left cisco-vpn.service
# permanently failed/start-limited — and its failure cleanup then deleted the pid
# file out from under a live session. /run is root-owned tmpfs; the unit keeps
# the dir with RuntimeDirectory(Preserve), connect_with_cookie creates it elsewhere.
RUN_DIR=/run/cisco-vpn
PID_FILE="$RUN_DIR/vpn.pid"
LOG_FILE="$STATE_DIR/vpn.log"
SESSION_FILE="$STATE_DIR/vpn.session"

# Watchdog state: tiny marker files next to the session. vpn.suspended = a human
# took the tunnel down on purpose, vpn.strikes = consecutive failed heals,
# vpn.blocked = the watchdog parked itself. The watchdog timer runs `heal` as root
# with HOME pointed here, so anything root creates is handed straight back to the
# home's owner — or the user could no longer manage (or even append to) their own
# state files afterwards.
own_state() {
  [ "$(id -u)" -eq 0 ] || return 0
  local u g
  u="$(stat -c %u "$HOME" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  { [ -n "$u" ] && [ "$u" != 0 ]; } || return 0
  g="$(stat -c %g "$HOME" 2>/dev/null || echo "$u")"
  chown "$u:$g" "$@" 2>/dev/null || true
}
mark()   { mkdir -p "$STATE_DIR"; printf '%s\n' "${2:-}" > "$STATE_DIR/$1"; own_state "$STATE_DIR" "$STATE_DIR/$1"; }
unmark() { rm -f "$STATE_DIR/$1" 2>/dev/null || $SUDO rm -f "$STATE_DIR/$1" 2>/dev/null || true; }
marked() { [ -e "$STATE_DIR/$1" ]; }

save_session() {   # <host> <fingerprint> <cookie> <gateway-as-typed>
  mkdir -p "$STATE_DIR"
  own_state "$STATE_DIR"
  # Create empty and lock down BEFORE writing: this file holds a live VPN session.
  ( umask 077; : > "$SESSION_FILE" )
  {
    printf 'HOST=%q\n'        "$1"
    printf 'FINGERPRINT=%q\n' "$2"
    printf 'COOKIE=%q\n'      "$3"
    printf 'GATEWAY=%q\n'     "$4"
    # Route narrowing travels WITH the session, so a later resume — the watchdog's
    # included — reconnects with the same footprint instead of a full tunnel.
    printf 'SAVED_ROUTES=%q\n'  "$ROUTES"
    printf 'SAVED_VPN_DNS=%q\n' "$VPN_DNS"
  } > "$SESSION_FILE"
  own_state "$SESSION_FILE"
  # A fresh login invalidates whatever made the watchdog park itself.
  unmark vpn.blocked; unmark vpn.strikes; unmark vpn.suspended; unmark vpn.heal-log; unmark vpn.dns
}

# Sets VPN_PID_SOURCE so callers can tell "the session we started" from "some
# openconnect that happens to be running".
VPN_PID_SOURCE=""
vpn_pid() {
  local p=""
  if [ -r "$PID_FILE" ]; then
    p="$(head -1 "$PID_FILE" 2>/dev/null | tr -dc '0-9')"
    # A stale pid file could otherwise point at whatever process later reused the id.
    if [ -n "$p" ] && grep -qa openconnect "/proc/$p/cmdline" 2>/dev/null; then
      VPN_PID_SOURCE=pidfile; printf '%s' "$p"; return 0
    fi
  fi
  # A foreground session (no -b) writes no pid file at all, and `status` from a second
  # terminal would then report "not connected" over a perfectly live tunnel.
  p="$(pgrep -x openconnect 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
  [ -n "$p" ] || return 1
  VPN_PID_SOURCE=scan; printf '%s' "$p"
}

# Wait for a signalled openconnect to actually go away, and say so if it does not —
# reporting "suspended" over a still-live tunnel is worse than reporting nothing.
await_exit() {  # <pid> <what>
  local pid="$1" what="$2" i
  for i in $(seq 1 10); do kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null || return 0; sleep 1; done
  echo "WARN: pid $pid did not exit after $what — check 'cisco-vpn status'" >&2
  return 1
}

# The tunnel openconnect built. Named tun0 in practice, but never assume it.
vpn_dev() { ip -o link show type tun 2>/dev/null | awk -F': ' 'NR==1{print $2}'; }

# Both cookie paths (sso and `cookie`) connect identically — cookie on stdin, and a
# UA/version pair mirroring openconnect-sso's, so version-checking DAP policies see a
# client they know.
connect_with_cookie() {
  local host="$1" fp="${2:-}"
  setup_routes
  # --force-dpd: most "up but nothing flows" states (host slept, NAT forgot us) are
  # exactly what dead-peer detection exists for — openconnect notices within 30s and
  # reconnects itself on the same cookie. The heal watchdog covers what DPD misses.
  set -- --cookie-on-stdin --useragent 'AnyConnect Linux_64 4.7.00136' --version-string 4.7.00136 --force-dpd 30
  [ -n "$fp" ] && set -- "$@" --servercert "$fp"
  if [ "$BACKGROUND" = 1 ]; then
    if vpn_pid >/dev/null; then
      echo "cisco-vpn: already connected (pid $(vpn_pid)) — 'cisco-vpn stop' first" >&2
      return 1
    fi
    mkdir -p "$STATE_DIR"
    own_state "$STATE_DIR"
    # The redirect is opened by THIS shell, so the log stays owned by us and readable
    # without sudo even though openconnect goes on to run as root. When a root-run
    # heal/boot resume is the first to create it, hand it to the user for the same
    # reason (a later user-run `sso -b` must be able to append).
    ( umask 077; : >> "$LOG_FILE" )
    own_state "$LOG_FILE"
    $SUDO install -d -m 0755 "$RUN_DIR"
    # -b forks only after the tunnel is up, so a non-zero exit here is a real failure.
    $SUDO openconnect --protocol=anyconnect -b --pid-file "$PID_FILE" \
      ${ROUTE_ARGS+"${ROUTE_ARGS[@]}"} "$@" "$host" >>"$LOG_FILE" 2>&1
    sleep 1
    vpn_pid >/dev/null || { echo "cisco-vpn: failed to start — see $LOG_FILE" >&2; return 1; }
    echo "==> cisco-vpn: connected in the background (pid $(vpn_pid)); cisco-vpn status"
  else
    # Ctrl-C (or TERM) on a foreground session is a server-side LOGOUT — the
    # cookie burns with it. When this session is the saved one (sso/resume set
    # the flag), drop the file too, or resume and the watchdog would keep
    # retrying a cookie that can never work again.
    local st=0
    [ "$SESSION_IS_CURRENT" = 1 ] && trap 'rm -f "$SESSION_FILE"' INT TERM
    $SUDO openconnect --protocol=anyconnect ${ROUTE_ARGS+"${ROUTE_ARGS[@]}"} "$@" "$host" || st=$?
    trap - INT TERM
    return "$st"
  fi
}

human_bytes() {
  awk -v b="${1:-0}" 'BEGIN{
    split("B KiB MiB GiB TiB", u, " "); i = 1
    while (b >= 1024 && i < 5) { b /= 1024; i++ }
    printf (i == 1 ? "%d %s" : "%.1f %s"), b, u[i]
  }'
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ tunnel health
# The failure all of this exists for: openconnect alive, tun0 present, routes
# installed — and nothing flows (a host sleep is the usual cause). From the outside
# that is indistinguishable from healthy, so the only real test is a probe THROUGH
# the tunnel; the fix is what you would type by hand, suspend && resume.
PROBE_TIMEOUT=5
PROBE_FILE="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/dotenv/vpn-probe"
UNIT_FILE=/etc/systemd/system/cisco-vpn.service

probe_tcp() {  # <host> <port> — completing the handshake is the whole test, and a
               # hostname also exercises DNS resolution inside the VM on the way
  timeout "$PROBE_TIMEOUT" bash -c 'exec 3<>"/dev/tcp/$0/$1"' "$1" "$2" 2>/dev/null
}
probe_url() {  # transport is the test: any HTTP status counts, including 401/503.
               # -k because reaching a self-signed intranet box still proves flow.
  curl -ks -o /dev/null --noproxy '*' --max-time "$PROBE_TIMEOUT" "$1" 2>/dev/null
}
probe_dns() {  # <server> — any DNS response, REFUSED included, proves packets flow
               # both ways (dig exits 0 on a response, 9 on silence). Without dig,
               # a TCP connect to port 53 — not UDP, but the same path.
  if have dig; then
    dig +time=3 +tries=1 "@$1" . soa >/dev/null 2>&1
  else
    probe_tcp "$1" 53
  fi
}

# The resolvers the VPN pushed are the one probe target that needs no configuration.
# Both sources are filtered through `ip route get`: a nameserver NOT routed via the
# tunnel (127.0.0.53, the LAN resolver, or DNS outside a --routes restriction) says
# nothing about the tunnel and would only produce false heals.
vpn_dns_servers() {  # <tun-dev>
  local dev="$1" cand="" ns
  [ -n "$dev" ] || return 0
  if have resolvectl; then
    cand="$(resolvectl dns "$dev" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^[^:]*: *//' | tr ' ' '\n' | grep -E '^[0-9a-fA-F:.]+$' || true)"
  fi
  [ -n "$cand" ] || cand="$(awk '/^nameserver/{print $2}' /etc/resolv.conf 2>/dev/null || true)"
  for ns in $cand; do
    if ip route get "$ns" 2>/dev/null | grep -qE " dev $dev( |\$)"; then printf '%s\n' "$ns"; fi
  done
  return 0
}

# Fills PROBES (dns:<ip> | host[:port] | URL) and PROBE_SOURCE.
collect_probes() {
  PROBES=(); PROBE_SOURCE=""
  local line ns
  if [ -n "${CISCO_VPN_PROBE:-}" ]; then
    for line in ${CISCO_VPN_PROBE//,/ }; do PROBES+=("$line"); done
    PROBE_SOURCE='$CISCO_VPN_PROBE'
  elif [ -r "$PROBE_FILE" ]; then
    while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
      line="${line%%#*}"
      line="${line#"${line%%[![:space:]]*}"}"; line="${line%"${line##*[![:space:]]}"}"
      if [ -n "$line" ]; then PROBES+=("$line"); fi
    done < "$PROBE_FILE"
    PROBE_SOURCE="$PROBE_FILE"
  fi
  if [ ${#PROBES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then
    for ns in $(vpn_dns_servers "$(vpn_dev)"); do PROBES+=("dns:$ns"); done
    PROBE_SOURCE="VPN-pushed DNS"
  fi
  return 0
}

probe_one() {
  local h
  case "$1" in
    http://*|https://*) probe_url "$1" ;;
    dns:*)              probe_dns "${1#dns:}" ;;
    \[*\]:*)            h="${1%%\]*}"; probe_tcp "${h#\[}" "${1##*\]:}" ;;  # [v6]:port
    \[*\])              h="${1%\]}";   probe_tcp "${h#\[}" 443 ;;
    *:*:*)              probe_tcp "$1" 443 ;;  # bare IPv6 — 2+ colons can't be host:port
    *:*)                probe_tcp "${1%:*}" "${1##*:}" ;;
    *)                  probe_tcp "$1" 443 ;;
  esac
}

# Sets HEALTH_MSG; returns 0 traffic flows | 1 wedged | 2 not connected | 3 nothing
# to probe with. Any single target answering means healthy — one dead intranet host
# must not read as a dead tunnel.
run_health() {
  HEALTH_MSG=""
  local pid dev t up
  pid="$(vpn_pid || true)"
  [ -n "$pid" ] || { HEALTH_MSG="not connected"; return 2; }
  dev="$(vpn_dev)"
  if [ -z "$dev" ]; then
    # No tun device YET is normal for minutes during the browser-SSO dance; only
    # an old process with no device is a wedge. Without this grace the watchdog
    # would SIGHUP a login in progress.
    up="$(ps -o etimes= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"
    if [ -n "$up" ] && [ "$up" -lt 600 ]; then
      HEALTH_MSG="openconnect up ${up}s with no tun device yet — still connecting/authenticating"
      return 3
    fi
    HEALTH_MSG="openconnect runs (pid $pid) but no tun device exists"
    return 1
  fi
  # DNS-config loss: resolved forgets tun0's per-link DNS when it restarts, and
  # the legacy file path could be rewritten behind our back (resolved regenerating
  # the stub file vpnc-script wrote through the /etc/resolv.conf symlink). The
  # tunnel still FLOWS either way, so probes alone read as healthy while every
  # corporate lookup fails. vpn.dns is written by the connect shim iff the server
  # pushed resolvers and we kept them; none of them still configured = lost.
  # rc 1 makes heal suspend+resume, and the reconnect re-registers the DNS.
  if [ -s "$STATE_DIR/vpn.dns" ]; then
    local cfg ns lost=1
    cfg="$( { have resolvectl && resolvectl dns "$dev" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^[^:]*: *//'
              awk '/^nameserver/{print $2}' /etc/resolv.conf 2>/dev/null; } | tr ' ' '\n' )"
    for ns in $(cat "$STATE_DIR/vpn.dns"); do
      printf '%s\n' "$cfg" | grep -qxF -- "$ns" && lost=0
    done
    if [ "$lost" = 1 ]; then
      HEALTH_MSG="tunnel up but the VPN DNS is gone (resolved restarted, or resolv.conf was rewritten) — a reconnect re-registers it"
      return 1
    fi
  fi
  collect_probes
  if [ ${#PROBES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then
    HEALTH_MSG="no probe target — the VPN pushed no in-tunnel DNS and $PROBE_FILE is absent"
    return 3
  fi
  for t in "${PROBES[@]}"; do
    if probe_one "$t"; then
      HEALTH_MSG="$t answers through $dev (targets: $PROBE_SOURCE)"
      return 0
    fi
  done
  HEALTH_MSG="tunnel is up but nothing flows — no answer from: ${PROBES[*]} ($PROBE_SOURCE)"
  return 1
}

# Is the HEAD-END reachable over the public internet? When it is not, the uplink
# is down (Mac asleep, cable out) — a reconnect cannot help and failing one must
# not count as a strike; the watchdog just waits. Fail-open: no session, no veto.
gateway_reachable() {
  local h p=443
  [ -r "$SESSION_FILE" ] || return 0
  # Subshell so sourcing does not clobber HOST/GATEWAY in the caller.
  h="$( . "$SESSION_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true; printf '%s' "${HOST:-${GATEWAY:-}}" )"
  h="${h#https://}"; h="${h%%/*}"
  case "$h" in
    *:*:*) h="[$h]" ;;                   # IPv6 literal — default port, URL form
    *:*)   p="${h##*:}"; h="${h%:*}" ;;
  esac
  [ -n "$h" ] || return 0
  # NOT a bare TCP probe: under Lima's user-mode networking the proxy accepts
  # every SYN locally before dialing upstream, so connect() succeeds even with
  # the uplink down. An HTTP(S) response cannot be faked that way — and the
  # head-end answers on its AnyConnect port even unauthenticated.
  probe_url "https://$h:$p/"
}

record_strike() {  # <why> — 3 in a row parks the --auto watchdog until a human acts
  local n
  n="$(tr -dc '0-9' < "$STATE_DIR/vpn.strikes" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  n=$(( ${n:-0} + 1 ))
  mark vpn.strikes "$n"
  echo "cisco-vpn heal: reconnect did not restore traffic (${1:-?}) — strike $n/3" >&2
  if [ "${AUTO:-0}" = 1 ] && [ "$n" -ge 3 ]; then
    mark vpn.blocked "3 failed heals in a row ($(date '+%F %T')) — session expired server-side?"
    echo "cisco-vpn heal: parking the watchdog — 'cisco-vpn heal' or a new sso login resets it" >&2
  fi
}

# One reconnect: suspend semantics (SIGHUP keeps the cookie), resume from the saved
# session, then probe until traffic actually flows again. Exits — never returns.
attempt_reconnect() {
  local pid ok=1 i vrc now log cnt
  if [ "${AUTO:-0}" = 1 ]; then
    # Rate limit: 3 auto-reconnects per rolling hour. A tunnel that keeps
    # "re-wedging" minutes after a verified-good heal is almost always a flaky
    # probe target — and every bounce is a real disconnect. Waiting is not
    # parking: the window frees itself, and a manual heal ignores it.
    log="$STATE_DIR/vpn.heal-log"; cnt=0
    now="$(date +%s)"
    if [ -r "$log" ]; then
      awk -v n="$now" '$1 > n-3600' "$log" > "$log.new" 2>/dev/null && mv -f "$log.new" "$log"
      cnt="$(wc -l < "$log" 2>/dev/null | tr -dc '0-9')"
    fi
    if [ "${cnt:-0}" -ge 3 ]; then
      echo "cisco-vpn heal: 3 auto-reconnects in the last hour — waiting instead of bouncing again" >&2
      echo "                (wedges this frequent usually mean a flaky probe target: check $PROBE_FILE)" >&2
      exit 0
    fi
    printf '%s\n' "$now" >> "$log"; own_state "$log"
  fi
  pid="$(vpn_pid || true)"
  if have systemctl && [ -e "$UNIT_FILE" ]; then
    # Go through cisco-vpn.service: its KillSignal=SIGHUP makes `restart` exactly
    # suspend+resume, and the new openconnect lands in THAT unit's cgroup. Resuming
    # directly from the oneshot timer service would leave openconnect in the
    # timer's cgroup, which systemd tears down with SIGTERM when the run ends —
    # and SIGTERM is a logout, burning the very cookie this is built around.
    if [ -n "$pid" ] && ! systemctl is-active --quiet cisco-vpn.service; then
      # An openconnect the unit does not know about (foreground start, manual
      # resume): take it down ourselves or ExecStart sees "already connected".
      $SUDO kill -HUP "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
      await_exit "$pid" SIGHUP || true
      $SUDO rm -f "$PID_FILE"
    fi
    # A start-limited unit refuses to restart until its counter clears. The
    # watchdog owns give-up policy (strikes + the hourly cap above) — and it only
    # gets here once the gateway is actually reachable — so clear systemd's.
    $SUDO systemctl reset-failed cisco-vpn.service 2>/dev/null || true
    $SUDO systemctl restart cisco-vpn.service || ok=0
  elif [ "${AUTO:-0}" = 1 ]; then
    echo "cisco-vpn heal: no $UNIT_FILE — refusing a timer-run reconnect (see above:" >&2
    echo "                the cgroup teardown would log the session off). Run:" >&2
    echo "                cisco-vpn install-service" >&2
    record_strike "no systemd unit to reconnect through"
    exit 1
  else
    if [ -n "$pid" ]; then
      $SUDO kill -HUP "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
      await_exit "$pid" SIGHUP || exit 1
      $SUDO rm -f "$PID_FILE"
    fi
    "$0" resume --background || ok=0
  fi
  if [ "$ok" = 1 ]; then
    # Routes and DNS land a beat after the fork; give it three sweeps.
    for i in 1 2 3; do
      sleep 5
      vrc=0; run_health || vrc=$?
      case "$vrc" in
        0|3)
          unmark vpn.strikes; unmark vpn.blocked
          echo "==> cisco-vpn: healed — $HEALTH_MSG"
          exit 0 ;;
      esac
    done
  fi
  record_strike "$HEALTH_MSG"
  exit 1
}

# Consumed here rather than passed through, in every mode — hence the pre-parse.
ROUTES="${CISCO_VPN_ROUTES:-}"
VPN_DNS="${CISCO_VPN_DNS:-on}"
BACKGROUND="${CISCO_VPN_BACKGROUND:-0}"
SESSION_IS_CURRENT=0   # sso/resume set it: "the saved session IS this connection"
_args=()
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --routes)       ROUTES="${2:?--routes needs a value}"; shift 2 ;;
    --routes=*)     ROUTES="${1#*=}"; shift ;;
    --no-vpn-dns)   VPN_DNS=off; shift ;;
    -b|--background) BACKGROUND=1; shift ;;
    --foreground)   BACKGROUND=0; shift ;;
    *)              _args+=("$1"); shift ;;
  esac
done
set -- ${_args+"${_args[@]}"}

install_openconnect() {
  if have apt-get; then
    $SUDO apt-get update -qq || true
    # DEBIAN_FRONTEND via `env`, not `export`: sudo's env_reset strips exported vars,
    # so the export form silently never reaches apt/dpkg/needrestart under sudo.
    # vpnc-scripts is a hard dep of the deb, but name it: it owns routes/DNS setup.
    $SUDO env DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y -qq openconnect vpnc-scripts \
      || { echo "cisco-vpn: apt install of openconnect failed" >&2; exit 1; }
  else
    echo "cisco-vpn: apt-get not found; this installer targets Debian/Ubuntu" >&2
    echo "           (any openconnect >= 9.00 with vpnc-script works — install manually)" >&2
    exit 1
  fi

  # Probe the capability, not just the version: SSO needs --external-browser
  # (openconnect >= 9.00; Ubuntu 22.04 still ships 8.20). No usable binary
  # fallback exists, so say so now instead of failing mid-login later.
  # NB: `openconnect --help` exits 1 by design — don't test it through pipefail.
  case "$(openconnect --help 2>&1 || true)" in
    *--external-browser*) ;;
    *)
      echo "WARN: this openconnect ($(openconnect --version 2>/dev/null | head -1)) lacks" >&2
      echo "      --external-browser — SSO needs >= 9.00 (Ubuntu 24.04+: 9.12, Debian 12+: 9.01+)." >&2
      ;;
  esac

  # Lima kernels ship tun built-in; probe anyway so a missing module fails here,
  # with a name, and not mid-connect.
  [ -e /dev/net/tun ] || $SUDO modprobe tun 2>/dev/null \
    || echo "WARN: /dev/net/tun missing — openconnect cannot create the tunnel" >&2

  echo "==> cisco-vpn: $(openconnect --version | head -1) ready"
  echo "    connect with: cisco-vpn <vpn-gateway>"
}

case "${1:-}" in
  -h|--help) usage 0 ;;
  install)   install_openconnect; exit 0 ;;

  status)
    pid="$(vpn_pid || true)"
    if [ -z "$pid" ]; then
      echo "cisco-vpn: not connected"
      if [ -r "$SESSION_FILE" ]; then
        # shellcheck disable=SC1090
        . "$SESSION_FILE"
        echo "    a saved session for ${GATEWAY:-${HOST:-?}} is present — cisco-vpn resume"
      fi
      if marked vpn.blocked; then
        echo "    watchdog parked: $(cat "$STATE_DIR/vpn.blocked" 2>/dev/null)"
        echo "    retry: cisco-vpn heal — or redo the login: cisco-vpn sso <gateway>"
      fi
      if marked vpn.suspended; then
        echo "    deliberately suspended — the watchdog leaves it down (cisco-vpn resume undoes)"
      fi
      exit 1
    fi
    dev="$(vpn_dev)"
    up="$(ps -o etimes= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"
    printf '==> cisco-vpn: connected (pid %s%s)\n' "$pid" \
      "${up:+, up $(printf '%dh%02dm%02ds' $((up/3600)) $((up%3600/60)) $((up%60)))}"
    [ -r "$SESSION_FILE" ] && { . "$SESSION_FILE"; printf '    %-10s %s\n' gateway "${GATEWAY:-${HOST:-?}}"; }
    if [ -n "$dev" ]; then
      printf '    %-10s %s  %s  mtu %s\n' interface "$dev" \
        "$(ip -o -4 addr show dev "$dev" 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $4}' | paste -sd, -)" \
        "$(cat "/sys/class/net/$dev/mtu" 2>/dev/null)"
      printf '    %-10s %s\n' routes "$(ip -o route show dev "$dev" 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1}' | paste -sd' ' - )"
      printf '    %-10s down %s   up %s\n' traffic \
        "$(human_bytes "$(cat "/sys/class/net/$dev/statistics/rx_bytes" 2>/dev/null)")" \
        "$(human_bytes "$(cat "/sys/class/net/$dev/statistics/tx_bytes" 2>/dev/null)")"
    fi
    # In-tunnel resolvers first (split DNS registers them per-link, so resolv.conf
    # rightly keeps 127.0.0.53); the resolv.conf fallback covers the legacy path.
    vdns="$(vpn_dns_servers "$dev" | paste -sd' ' -)"
    printf '    %-10s %s\n' dns "${vdns:-$(awk '/^nameserver/{print $2}' /etc/resolv.conf 2>/dev/null | paste -sd' ' -)}"
    printf '    %-10s %s\n' session \
      "$([ -r "$SESSION_FILE" ] && echo 'saved (survives suspend/reboot)' || echo 'none saved')"
    if marked vpn.blocked; then
      printf '    %-10s %s\n' watchdog \
        "PARKED: $(cat "$STATE_DIR/vpn.blocked" 2>/dev/null) — retry: cisco-vpn heal, or redo the sso login"
    elif have systemctl && systemctl is-active --quiet cisco-vpn-watch.timer 2>/dev/null; then
      printf '    %-10s %s\n' watchdog 'on — probes through the tunnel every minute'
    else
      printf '    %-10s %s\n' watchdog 'off — cisco-vpn install-service adds the auto-heal timer'
    fi
    [ "$VPN_PID_SOURCE" = scan ] && \
      printf '    %-10s %s\n' note 'found by process scan — started in the foreground, or by something else'
    # openconnect answers SIGUSR1 by writing its own stats into the log it was
    # started with — richer than anything we can read from /sys (DTLS vs TLS, cipher,
    # compression, reconnect count). Only reachable for a backgrounded session.
    if [ "${2:-}" = "-v" ] || [ "${2:-}" = "--verbose" ]; then
      if $SUDO kill -USR1 "$pid" 2>/dev/null && [ -r "$LOG_FILE" ]; then
        sleep 1
        echo "    --- openconnect ---"
        tail -12 "$LOG_FILE" | sed 's/^/    /'
      else
        echo "    (no log — session was not started with --background)"
      fi
    fi
    exit 0
    ;;

  health)
    quiet=0; case "${2:-}" in -q|--quiet) quiet=1 ;; esac
    hrc=0; run_health || hrc=$?
    case "$hrc" in
      0)
        # A probe that answers is ground truth — clear a parked watchdog with it.
        unmark vpn.blocked; unmark vpn.strikes
        [ "$quiet" = 1 ] || echo "==> cisco-vpn: healthy — $HEALTH_MSG" ;;
      3)
        hrc=0   # connected and unprobeable is not a failure, just say how to get probes
        if [ "$quiet" != 1 ]; then
          echo "==> cisco-vpn: connected, but $HEALTH_MSG"
          echo "    echo 'intranet.example.com:443' > $PROBE_FILE   # host[:port], URL or dns:<ip>, one per line"
        fi ;;
      1) [ "$quiet" = 1 ] || echo "cisco-vpn: UNHEALTHY — $HEALTH_MSG" ;;
      2) [ "$quiet" = 1 ] || echo "cisco-vpn: not connected" ;;
    esac
    exit "$hrc"
    ;;

  heal)
    AUTO=0; case "${2:-}" in --auto) AUTO=1 ;; esac
    if [ "$AUTO" = 1 ] && marked vpn.blocked; then
      # Parked after 3 straight failures — almost always an expired session, which
      # no retry loop can fix, and every retry pokes the gateway. Stay quiet;
      # `status` explains, and any human action below resets it.
      exit 0
    fi
    if [ "$AUTO" = 0 ]; then
      # A human asking IS the reset switch — including for systemd's own start
      # limit, which a string of failed resumes will have tripped, and for the
      # hourly auto-reconnect cap.
      unmark vpn.blocked; unmark vpn.strikes; unmark vpn.heal-log
      if have systemctl && [ -e "$UNIT_FILE" ]; then
        $SUDO systemctl reset-failed cisco-vpn.service 2>/dev/null || true
      fi
    fi
    hrc=0; run_health || hrc=$?
    if [ "$hrc" = 1 ] && [ "$AUTO" = 1 ]; then
      # One blip (a probe target rebooting, one lost packet) must not bounce the
      # tunnel: only two failing sweeps 8s apart count as wedged.
      sleep 8
      hrc=0; run_health || hrc=$?
    fi
    case "$hrc" in
      0|3)
        unmark vpn.strikes
        if [ "$AUTO" = 1 ]; then
          # rc 3 means "connected but nothing to probe with" — the one state the
          # watchdog cannot judge. Say so in the journal instead of exiting mute,
          # or this blindness is invisible until someone reads the script.
          [ "$hrc" = 3 ] && echo "cisco-vpn heal: skipping — $HEALTH_MSG"
        else
          echo "==> cisco-vpn: nothing to heal — $HEALTH_MSG"
        fi
        exit 0 ;;
      2)
        if [ "$AUTO" = 0 ]; then
          echo "cisco-vpn: not connected — use 'cisco-vpn resume' (or sso <gateway>)" >&2
          exit 1
        fi
        marked vpn.suspended && exit 0     # a deliberate suspend is not a fault
        [ -r "$SESSION_FILE" ] || exit 0   # nothing to resume with
        # 'systemctl stop cisco-vpn' is a suspend (KillSignal=SIGHUP) that writes
        # no marker — respect it. And a crashed unit is systemd's to retry first
        # (Restart=). Only a unit systemd has GIVEN UP on (failed, usually
        # start-limit-hit) is the watchdog's to pick up.
        if have systemctl && [ -e "$UNIT_FILE" ] && ! systemctl is-failed --quiet cisco-vpn.service; then
          exit 0
        fi
        # Settle, then look again: an sso login finishing this very second starts
        # its own openconnect, and suspend writes its marker just before teardown.
        sleep 2
        vpn_pid >/dev/null && exit 0
        marked vpn.suspended && exit 0
        if ! gateway_reachable; then
          echo "cisco-vpn heal: gateway unreachable — uplink is down, not the tunnel; waiting"
          exit 0
        fi
        echo "cisco-vpn heal: not connected but a session is saved — reconnecting"
        attempt_reconnect ;;
      1)
        if [ ! -r "$SESSION_FILE" ]; then
          # Reconnecting means resume, and resume needs the saved cookie. Killing
          # a wedged session we cannot restore would only make things worse.
          echo "cisco-vpn heal: wedged, but no saved session to reconnect from —" >&2
          echo "                reconnect by hand ('cisco-vpn sso <gateway> -b' saves one)" >&2
          [ "$AUTO" = 1 ] && exit 0
          exit 1
        fi
        if [ "$AUTO" = 1 ] && ! gateway_reachable; then
          echo "cisco-vpn heal: $HEALTH_MSG — but the gateway itself is unreachable, so the"
          echo "                uplink is down and a reconnect cannot help; waiting, no strike"
          exit 0
        fi
        echo "==> cisco-vpn heal: $HEALTH_MSG"
        attempt_reconnect ;;
    esac
    ;;

  stop)
    pid="$(vpn_pid || true)"
    if [ -z "$pid" ]; then
      echo "cisco-vpn: not connected"
      if [ -r "$SESSION_FILE" ]; then
        # stop means "logged out": even with nothing to signal, forget the saved
        # session so neither resume nor the watchdog resurrects it (server-side
        # the cookie expires on its own).
        rm -f "$SESSION_FILE"
        echo "    discarded the saved session"
      fi
      unmark vpn.blocked; unmark vpn.strikes; unmark vpn.suspended; unmark vpn.heal-log; unmark vpn.dns
      exit 0
    fi
    # SIGINT logs the session OFF, which is the honest meaning of "stop" — but it
    # also burns the cookie, so the saved session goes with it. Use suspend to keep it.
    $SUDO kill -INT "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
    await_exit "$pid" SIGINT || exit 1
    $SUDO rm -f "$PID_FILE"
    rm -f "$SESSION_FILE"
    unmark vpn.blocked; unmark vpn.strikes; unmark vpn.suspended; unmark vpn.heal-log; unmark vpn.dns
    echo "==> cisco-vpn: disconnected and logged out (saved session discarded)"
    exit 0
    ;;

  suspend)
    pid="$(vpn_pid || true)"
    if [ -z "$pid" ] && [ ! -r "$SESSION_FILE" ]; then echo "cisco-vpn: not connected"; exit 0; fi
    # Deliberate — the watchdog must not "fix" this by resuming it a minute later.
    # The marker goes FIRST: a heal --auto firing mid-teardown would otherwise see
    # "down, session saved, no marker" and resume the very tunnel being suspended.
    # An already-down tunnel still records the intent.
    mark vpn.suspended "$(date '+%F %T')"
    if [ -z "$pid" ]; then
      echo "==> cisco-vpn: already down — noted as deliberately suspended (resume undoes this)"
      exit 0
    fi
    # SIGHUP tears the tunnel down without logging off, so the cookie stays valid and
    # `cisco-vpn resume` can pick it up — after a reboot included.
    $SUDO kill -HUP "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
    if ! await_exit "$pid" SIGHUP; then
      unmark vpn.suspended   # still up — do not tell the watchdog otherwise
      exit 1
    fi
    $SUDO rm -f "$PID_FILE"
    echo "==> cisco-vpn: suspended, session kept — reconnect with: cisco-vpn resume"
    exit 0
    ;;

  resume)
    [ -r "$SESSION_FILE" ] || { echo "cisco-vpn: no saved session — run 'cisco-vpn sso <gateway>'" >&2; exit 1; }
    vpn_pid >/dev/null && { unmark vpn.suspended; echo "cisco-vpn: already connected (pid $(vpn_pid))"; exit 0; }
    HOST=""; COOKIE=""; FINGERPRINT=""; GATEWAY=""; SAVED_ROUTES=""; SAVED_VPN_DNS=""
    # shellcheck disable=SC1090
    . "$SESSION_FILE"
    [ -n "$COOKIE" ] || { echo "cisco-vpn: saved session has no cookie" >&2; exit 1; }
    # A session saved before SAVED_ROUTES existed says nothing about its footprint:
    # resuming it would silently open a FULL tunnel (all traffic and DNS through
    # the VPN). Make that an explained error instead — this exact revert is what
    # turned every wedge into a total outage before the fields were added.
    if [ -z "$ROUTES" ] && ! grep -q '^SAVED_ROUTES=' "$SESSION_FILE"; then
      echo "cisco-vpn: this saved session predates route saving — a resume would open a full tunnel." >&2
      echo "           Redo the login: cisco-vpn sso ${GATEWAY:-<gateway>} [--routes <cidrs>]" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    have openconnect || install_openconnect
    # Re-apply the connect-time narrowing: a watchdog resume of a session created
    # with --routes/--no-vpn-dns must not silently widen into a full tunnel.
    # Explicit flags/env on THIS invocation still win.
    [ -n "$ROUTES" ] || ROUTES="$SAVED_ROUTES"
    [ "$VPN_DNS" = off ] || VPN_DNS="${SAVED_VPN_DNS:-$VPN_DNS}"
    # Resuming ends a deliberate suspend, and a human retry also unparks the
    # watchdog. vpn.strikes stays — only a VERIFIED probe clears it, otherwise the
    # unit's ExecStart(=resume) would reset the counter on every failed heal.
    unmark vpn.suspended; unmark vpn.blocked
    SESSION_IS_CURRENT=1
    echo "==> cisco-vpn: resuming ${GATEWAY:-$HOST} from the saved session"
    # A cookie the head-end has already expired or logged out fails here; that is not
    # recoverable without a fresh browser login, so say so plainly.
    if ! printf '%s' "$COOKIE" | connect_with_cookie "$HOST" "$FINGERPRINT"; then
      echo "cisco-vpn: resume failed — the session likely expired server-side." >&2
      echo "           Run: cisco-vpn sso ${GATEWAY:-<gateway>}" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    exit 0
    ;;

  install-service)
    # Without this, a reboot is exactly the case the saved session CANNOT survive:
    # systemd stops everything with SIGTERM, openconnect treats that as a clean
    # shutdown and logs the session off, and the cookie is dead before you come back.
    # KillSignal=SIGHUP is the whole point of the unit.
    have systemctl || { echo "cisco-vpn: no systemd on this box" >&2; exit 1; }
    # Under `sudo cisco-vpn install-service` $HOME is /root, and all three units
    # would bake HOME=/root in — a watchdog that can never find the session.
    if [ "$(id -u)" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "${SUDO_USER:-}" ]; then
      echo "cisco-vpn: run install-service as your user, not under sudo (it sudos internally)" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    wunit=/etc/systemd/system/cisco-vpn-watch.service
    wtimer=/etc/systemd/system/cisco-vpn-watch.timer
    self="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)/$(basename "$0")"
    $SUDO tee "$UNIT_FILE" >/dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=cisco-vpn — resume the saved openconnect session
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target
# An expired cookie can never resume, and Restart= would otherwise redo that
# failing login every 30s forever. Five tries in ten minutes, then stay failed —
# the watchdog picks a failed unit up (with its own strike/rate limits, and only
# once the gateway is actually reachable), as does a manual 'cisco-vpn heal'.
StartLimitIntervalSec=10min
StartLimitBurst=5

[Service]
# forking + PIDFile so systemd tracks openconnect itself (started with -b), not the
# wrapper script — that is what makes the signal below land on the right process.
# The pid file must live under /run: systemd refuses to trust a root service's
# PIDFile that traverses user-owned directories ("unsafe symlink chain"), and that
# refusal makes every start end failed (result: protocol) with the tunnel up.
# Preserve= so a stop/failure cleanup cannot delete the pid file of a session that
# was started outside the unit.
Type=forking
PIDFile=$PID_FILE
RuntimeDirectory=cisco-vpn
RuntimeDirectoryPreserve=yes
Environment=HOME=$HOME
ExecStart=$self resume --background
# SIGTERM would log the session off and leave nothing to resume. SIGHUP disconnects
# and keeps it. It also makes 'systemctl restart' literally suspend+resume, which
# is exactly how 'cisco-vpn heal' reconnects a wedged tunnel.
KillSignal=SIGHUP
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=30

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF
    # The watchdog: a oneshot probe, minutely. It never runs openconnect itself —
    # a heal goes through 'systemctl restart cisco-vpn' (see attempt_reconnect for
    # why that cgroup detail is load-bearing) — so oneshot teardown is safe here.
    $SUDO tee "$wunit" >/dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=cisco-vpn — probe through the tunnel, suspend+resume it if wedged

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=HOME=$HOME
ExecStart=$self heal --auto
EOF
    $SUDO tee "$wtimer" >/dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=cisco-vpn — minutely tunnel health check

[Timer]
OnBootSec=2min
OnUnitActiveSec=1min
AccuracySec=15s

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
EOF
    $SUDO systemctl daemon-reload
    $SUDO systemctl enable cisco-vpn.service >/dev/null 2>&1
    $SUDO systemctl enable --now cisco-vpn-watch.timer >/dev/null 2>&1
    cat <<EOF
==> cisco-vpn: installed $UNIT_FILE (resume at boot)
    and cisco-vpn-watch.timer (minutely: probe through the tunnel, heal if wedged).

    Log in once (cisco-vpn sso <gateway> -b) so a session exists; from then on a
    reboot suspends rather than logs out, the tunnel comes back on its own, and the
    "up but nothing loads" state a host sleep leaves behind is suspend+resumed for
    you. Sessions still expire server-side — the watchdog then parks itself after
    3 failed heals and 'cisco-vpn status' tells you to redo the SSO login.

      systemctl status cisco-vpn               what systemd thinks
      systemctl list-timers 'cisco-vpn-*'      when the next probe fires
      cisco-vpn status                         what the tunnel is actually doing
      cisco-vpn health                         one probe, right now
      sudo systemctl disable --now cisco-vpn cisco-vpn-watch.timer    undo
EOF
    exit 0
    ;;

  sso)
    # Embedded-SAML head-ends ("No SSO handler") — still entirely from this VM.
    # openconnect-saml --headless supplies the webview openconnect lacks, and waits on
    # a local callback server. But in EMBEDDED mode nothing ever redirects there: the
    # ASA ends the flow on its own page (sso-v2-login-final) and leaves the token in
    # the *browser* as the cookie named by sso-v2-token-cookie-name (acSamlv2Token on
    # HSG). Verified against vpn.unisg.ch: no <sso-v2-browser-mode> is ever sent, so
    # the callback can only be fed from the browser side — hence the relay below. The
    # cookie is not HttpOnly, so the extension (or bookmarklet, or you) can post it
    # to the callback, which Lima forwards into this VM. openconnect-saml then does
    # the auth-reply POST that swaps the token for the real webvpn session cookie.
    shift
    [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "cisco-vpn: sso mode needs <gateway>" >&2; usage 2 >&2; }
    gw="$1"; shift
    have openconnect-saml || {
      echo "cisco-vpn: openconnect-saml missing — install it with:" >&2
      echo "           sudo apt-get install -y pipx && pipx install openconnect-saml" >&2
      echo "           (base package only: the [gui]/[chrome] extras need a display)" >&2
      exit 1
    }
    have openconnect || { echo "==> cisco-vpn: openconnect missing — installing first"; install_openconnect; }

    creds="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/cisco-vpn-creds.XXXXXX")"   # mktemp -> 0600
    CLEANUP_FILES+=("$creds")
    # PYTHONUNBUFFERED: Python block-buffers when stdout is a pipe, which would hide
    # the login URL until after the callback had already timed out. Backgrounded so
    # we can relay a pasted token while its callback server is still listening; no
    # --user is passed, so it never competes with us for the terminal.
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 openconnect-saml --server "$gw" --headless --authenticate "$@" >"$creds" 2>&1 &
    saml_pid=$!

    # Wait for the login URL (and the callback port it chose — 29786 unless taken).
    login_url=""; cb_url=""
    for _ in $(seq 1 60); do
      kill -0 "$saml_pid" 2>/dev/null || break
      login_url="$(grep -oE 'https://[^ ]+saml[^ ]*' "$creds" | head -1 || true)"
      cb_url="$(grep -oE 'http://127\.0\.0\.1:[0-9]+' "$creds" | head -1 || true)"
      [ -n "$login_url" ] && [ -n "$cb_url" ] && break
      sleep 1
    done
    cb_port="${cb_url##*:}"

    if [ -n "$login_url" ]; then
      sso_osc52="$(command -v osc52-copy || true)"
      [ -n "$sso_osc52" ] && printf '%s' "$login_url" | "$sso_osc52" 2>/dev/null || true
      cat <<EOF

=========================== SSO login (embedded SAML) ===========================
Waiting for the token on callback port $cb_port.

1. Open this URL in a browser ON YOUR MAC (it is also on your clipboard):

   $login_url

2. Sign in. You land on a page saying you may close the tab — do NOT close it yet:
   the token is now a cookie there, and nothing sends it back automatically.
3. Getting the token back here, best first:
   a) NOTHING TO DO if the "Cisco VPN token relay" extension is loaded on the Mac
      (browser-extension/vpn-token-relay in this repo) — it posts the cookie itself.
   b) or click a bookmarklet saved on the Mac with this as its URL:

   javascript:(function(){var m=document.cookie.match(/acSamlv2Token=([^;]*)/);if(!m){alert('acSamlv2Token not found - are you on the vpn page?');return}location='http://localhost:$cb_port/callback?acSamlv2Token='+encodeURIComponent(m[1])})()

   c) or read it out (DevTools console: document.cookie) and paste it below.
================================================================================
EOF
      # Poll instead of a plain read: the bookmarklet may complete the login for us,
      # in which case openconnect-saml exits and there is nothing left to paste.
      token=""
      printf 'Waiting for the extension/bookmarklet — or paste acSamlv2Token here: '
      while kill -0 "$saml_pid" 2>/dev/null; do
        if read -r -t 5 token </dev/tty 2>/dev/null && [ -n "$token" ]; then
          curl -sG --data-urlencode "acSamlv2Token=$token" "$cb_url/callback" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
            && echo "==> token relayed to the local callback server" \
            || echo "WARN: could not reach $cb_url — did the login time out?" >&2
          break
        fi
      done
      echo
    fi

    wait "$saml_pid" || true
    # || true: grep exits 1 when it selects nothing, which pipefail would turn into
    # a silent abort right before the cookie is read.
    { grep -v -E '^(HOST|COOKIE|FINGERPRINT)=' "$creds" || true; } | tail -5

    # Values are shlex-quoted by openconnect-saml; eval is its documented consumption.
    eval "$(grep -E '^(HOST|COOKIE|FINGERPRINT)=' "$creds" || true)"
    [ -n "${COOKIE:-}" ] || { echo "cisco-vpn: no cookie returned — SSO did not complete" >&2; exit 1; }

    # Save before connecting: the whole point is to survive the tunnel dying, and a
    # crash between here and the connect would otherwise lose a perfectly good cookie.
    save_session "${HOST:-$gw}" "${FINGERPRINT:-}" "$COOKIE" "$gw"
    SESSION_IS_CURRENT=1
    echo "==> cisco-vpn: SSO complete — connecting to ${HOST:-$gw}"
    printf '%s' "$COOKIE" | connect_with_cookie "${HOST:-$gw}" "${FINGERPRINT:-}"
    exit $?
    ;;

  cookie)
    # Embedded-SAML escape hatch: the browser part already happened on the Mac
    # (openconnect-sso --authenticate printed HOST/COOKIE/FINGERPRINT); we only
    # consume the cookie here. The UA/version pair mirrors what openconnect-sso
    # itself connects with, so version-checking DAP policies see a known client.
    # NB: Ctrl-C logs the session out server-side and BURNS the cookie; a plain
    # network drop (or SIGHUP) keeps it reusable.
    shift
    [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "cisco-vpn: cookie mode needs <host> [fingerprint]" >&2; usage 2 >&2; }
    host="$1"; fp="${2:-}"
    have openconnect || { echo "==> cisco-vpn: openconnect missing — installing first"; install_openconnect; }
    echo "==> cisco-vpn: connecting to $host with handed-over cookie (Ctrl-C disconnects)"
    connect_with_cookie "$host" "$fp"      # cookie arrives on our stdin
    exit $?
    ;;
esac

# First non-flag arg is the gateway; anything else passes through to openconnect.
server="${CISCO_VPN_SERVER:-}"
if [ $# -gt 0 ] && [ "${1#-}" = "$1" ]; then server="$1"; shift; fi
[ -n "$server" ] || { echo "cisco-vpn: no gateway given (arg or \$CISCO_VPN_SERVER)" >&2; usage 2 >&2; }

have openconnect || { echo "==> cisco-vpn: openconnect missing — installing first"; install_openconnect; }

# The "browser" openconnect launches: print the URL + best-effort OSC 52 copy to the
# Mac clipboard. Generated fresh per run because openconnect execs it as root with no
# shell and outside our PATH — so it must be a real file with absolute paths baked in.
osc52="$(command -v osc52-copy || true)"
helper="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/cisco-vpn-browser.XXXXXX")"
CLEANUP_FILES+=("$helper")
cat >"$helper" <<EOF
#!/bin/sh
# Invoked by openconnect with the SSO URL as \$1 (stdout arrives on its stderr).
printf '\n================================ SSO login ================================\n'
printf 'Open this URL in a browser ON YOUR MAC (not in the VM):\n\n%s\n\n' "\$1"
printf 'Entra ID login runs there; the final http://localhost:29786/ redirect is\n'
printf 'forwarded by Lima back into this VM to complete authentication.\n'
printf '===========================================================================\n\n'
[ -x "$osc52" ] && printf '%s' "\$1" | "$osc52" 2>/dev/null \
  && printf '(URL is also on your Mac clipboard — OSC 52.)\n\n'
exit 0
EOF
chmod 755 "$helper"

# Troubleshooting:
#  - "No SSO handler"        -> head-end only allows *embedded* SAML (e.g. vpn.unisg.ch);
#                               use `cisco-vpn sso` instead.
#  - 404 right after connect -> some ASA DAP policies match the User-Agent; retry
#                               with: --useragent='AnyConnect Linux_64 4.10.05095'
#                               (openconnect's default already contains "AnyConnect").
#  - exits after hours up    -> the session cookie expired or the head-end ended the
#                               session; that's fatal by design (no headless token
#                               refresh) — rerun and redo the browser login. Brief
#                               network drops reconnect on their own.
setup_routes
echo "==> cisco-vpn: connecting to $server (Ctrl-C disconnects)"
$SUDO openconnect --protocol=anyconnect --external-browser="$helper" --force-dpd 30 \
  ${ROUTE_ARGS+"${ROUTE_ARGS[@]}"} "$@" "$server"
{{ end -}}
